        "text": "The meterstick’s proper length is 1.0 m, and it is measured as 0.60 m in the other spaceship’s frame, so the relative Lorentz factor is\n\n\\[\n\\gamma_{\\text{rel}}=\\frac{1.0}{0.60}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThus the relative speed between the ships is\n\n\\[\n\\gamma_{\\text{rel}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2/c^2}}=\\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n1-\\frac{u^2}{c^2}=\\frac{9}{25}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nu=0.8c.\n\\]\n\nFor two ships moving toward Earth with equal speed \\(v\\) in opposite directions, their relative speed is\n\n\\[\nu=\\frac{2v}{1+v^2/c^2}.\n\\]\n\nSet \\(u=0.8c\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2v}{1+v^2/c^2}=0.8c.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\beta=v/c\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\beta}{1+\\beta^2}=0.8\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2\\beta=0.8(1+\\beta^2)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2\\beta^2-5\\beta+2=0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\beta=\\frac{5\\pm3}{4}.\n\\]\n\nThe physical solution is\n\n\\[\n\\beta=0.5.\n\\]\n\nSo each spaceship moves at \\(0.5c\\) relative to Earth.\n\nAnswer: F",
